From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthoine Bourgeois <anthoine.bourgeois@vates.tech>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 13:54:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] vhd-util: add batmap, to convert a VHD to the blktap batmap
 layout

A dynamic or differencing VHD created by another tool (Hyper-V, qemu)
has no batmap, so tapdisk and coalesce read the sector bitmap of every
allocated block to know whether it is full, and libvhd keeps the
creator of the image as is.

Add "vhd-util batmap -n <vhd> [-d]", which converts the VHD in place:
the batmap is placed right after the BAT, where libvhd expects it. The
parent locators overlapping it are moved right after the batmap, still
before the data blocks, since libvhd takes all metadata to come first.
The data blocks overlapping the batmap or the moved locators are moved
past the end of data, with their data page-aligned. The batmap bit of
each block with a full sector bitmap is then set, and the footer
creator is switched to "tap" at the current version.

libvhd ignores the batmap as long as the creator is not "tap", so each
step is synced before the next: blocks are copied before the BAT points
to them, locators before the header points to them, and the batmap is
written before the footer is switched. On a file, the footer is first
written further out if the moved blocks would overwrite it; a block
device without the space needed is refused with -ENOSPC. The header is
still rewritten in place when locators move, VHD having no backup
header. A converted VHD is reported as such, and its backup footer is
updated if a previous run stopped before writing it.

Only 2 MiB blocks are supported, as the batmap checksum and size in
libvhd assume them. With -d, only the moves are printed.

Tested on qemu-img dynamic VHDs and on Microsoft-style differencing
VHDs, with pwrite() failures injected at each write: the VHD passes
vhd-util check, its contents are unchanged and a rerun completes the
conversion.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
